        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(mobile): clarify gateway connection security setup",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nManual gateway setup on Android and iOS used ambiguous `Local` and `TLS` controls. Users could enter a remote hostname while the app still targeted loopback, select transport security without understanding the effective endpoint, or receive protocol/TLS failures without a concrete recovery step.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nBoth mobile apps now:\n\n- present connection security as `Unencrypted` or `Secure (TLS)`\n- require secure transport for non-private hosts\n- preview the effective `ws://` or `wss://` endpoint without replacing user-facing labels with protocol syntax\n- provide actionable TLS guidance\n- provide a copyable `openclaw update` command for gateway protocol mismatch\n\nRemote-host security selection is deterministic local policy based on the entered host. It does not ping, probe, or otherwise preflight the gateway.\n\nThis PR is AI-assisted.\n\n## User Impact\n\nAndroid and iOS users get the same connection model and recovery guidance during onboarding and later manual gateway configuration. Remote connections can no longer silently retain a loopback-oriented mode, and error states explain the operator action needed to recover.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Android focused unit tests and debug build passed.\n- iOS focused tests passed: 23 tests, 0 failures.\n- Fresh pre-commit `$autoreview` completed with no actionable findings.\n- `git diff --check` passes on the rebased branch.\n- Before/after screenshots are attached in the [mobile UI evidence comment](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/101325#issuecomment-4899867668); no image assets are committed to the repository.\n",
